//@version=6
indicator("UCTPROBE_GB_ROUND_NEGPRECISION", overlay = false)

// ⭐⭐ ONE QUESTION, ALONE, BECAUSE ITS ANSWER IS A COMPILE FAILURE.
// Does `math.round(x, -2)` — round to the nearest hundred — exist at TradingView?
//
// ✅ ALREADY ANSWERED, 2026-09-11, AND THE ANSWER IS NO:
//     CE10041 — Invalid value "-2" for "precision" parameter of the
//     "math.round()" function. It must be between 0 and 100
// read off a live chart at line 34, columns 6-26 of the combined probe this line
// was split out of (`groupb-round-max-vwap.pine`).
//
// ⛔ THE FILE IS KEPT ANYWAY, AND NOT AS A CURIOSITY. A refusal measured once is a
// fact about the vendor ON THAT DAY; TradingView widens argument domains between
// releases, and the day `precision` accepts a negative value this file compiles
// and starts plotting instead of failing — which is a louder signal than anybody
// re-reading a note. It is the negative control for the `math.round` family.
//
// ⚠️ AND IT IS WHY THE COMBINED FILE NOW CARRIES A SCAR. Three documented-and-
// therefore-expected-to-compile questions shared one file; this one was not the
// risky arity anybody had flagged, and it took `math.max`'s variadic reading and
// `ta.vwap`'s anchoring reading down with it. "Expected to compile" is a
// prediction, and the grouping rule should read "one question per file unless the
// forms are proven" rather than "unless they are documented".

plot(bar_index, "N01_bar_index_CONTROL")                                    // N01
plot(math.round(1234.5, -2), "N02_round_negPRECISION_compiles_is_the_finding")  // N02
